## Layout

| Path                 | What it is                                                     |
| -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `definition.yaml`    | The plugin manifest: metadata, RBAC, console menu and components |
| `plugin.go`          | The plugin itself                                                |
| `main.go`            | Hands the plugin to the SDK runtime                              |
| `Justfile`           | Build, test, lint and image recipes                              |
| `mise.toml`          | Pinned tool versions (Go, just{{if .ConsoleVite}}, bun{{end}})                            |
| `LICENSE`            | {{.License}}                                                     |
{{- if .HasConsole}}
| `console.go`         | Embeds the console UI assets                                     |
| `console/`           | {{if .ConsoleVite}}Vite build output (generated){{else}}Console UI pages{{end}}                       |
{{- end}}
{{- if .ConsoleVite}}
| `console-ui/`        | Console UI source (TypeScript + Vite)                            |
{{- end}}

## Develop

Tool versions are pinned in `mise.toml`. With [mise](https://mise.jdx.dev)
installed, `mise install` gets Go, [just](https://just.systems){{if .ConsoleVite}} and
[bun](https://bun.sh){{end}} at the versions this project expects. Recipes live in the
`Justfile`; run `just --list` to see them all.

```shell
go mod tidy      # needs network access, or a warm module cache
just build
just test
```
{{if .ConsoleVite}}
The Go build embeds `console/`, which Vite produces from `console-ui/`:

```shell
cd console-ui && bun install && bun run build
git add bun.lock
```

Commit `bun.lock`, then change `bun install` to `bun install --frozen-lockfile`
in the Dockerfile so image builds are reproducible.

NLDD design-system styles are deliberately not bundled. The console host serves
them at run time from `/plugin-ui/`, so every plugin picks up design-system
updates without rebuilding.
{{end}}
```shell
just docker
```

## Fill in

- `definition.yaml`: `spec.permissions.rbac` decides what the plugin may do in
  the cluster. It is materialised verbatim as a ClusterRole, so grant only what
  the plugin uses.
{{- if .IsHelm}}
  The rules that ship are only what Helm itself needs to install a release;
  uncomment the commented blocks for the kinds of object your chart creates.
- `plugin.go`: point `repoURL`, `chartName` and `chartVersion` at the chart you
  install, and list its CRDs in `expectedCRDs`.
{{- end}}
{{- if .HasConsole}}
- `spec.menu`, `spec.customComponents`, `spec.allowedResources` and the files
  under `console/` are one contract. `definition_test.go` checks that the files
  referenced actually exist; nothing checks the rest, so change them together.
{{- end}}
- Every `TODO` marker in this project.

`metadata.license` says `{{.License}}`, and `LICENSE` holds its text. If that
file says TODO, `functl plugin create` had no text for the licence you chose --
paste it in, or re-create with one of MIT, Apache-2.0, GPL-3.0-only or EUPL-1.2.

## Publish

Publishing a standalone plugin is **not supported yet**. Today a plugin
definition is published from inside the Fundament monorepo with
`just plugin-publish`, which requires the plugin to live under `plugins/` and to
have a catalog entry in the appstore. Until `functl plugin publish` exists, you
can build and run this plugin, but not install it into a Fundament cluster.

